Package: iconic
Title: Causal Model Selection with Genetic Instruments and Negative
        Controls
Version: 0.9.5
biocViews: Statistics, Genetics, MultipleComparison, Regression,
        Transcriptomics, RNASeq, Bayesian
Author: Sean T. Bresnahan [aut, cre], Charis Xiong [aut]
Maintainer: Sean T. Bresnahan <stbresnahan@mdanderson.org>
Authors@R: c(
    person("Sean T.", "Bresnahan", role = c("aut", "cre"),
        email = "stbresnahan@mdanderson.org"),
    person("Charis", "Xiong", role = "aut",
        email = "charis.xiong@gmail.com"))
Description: Provides a model selection workflow for causal inference with
    genetic instruments and negative controls in observational omics data.
    The workflow auto-trains a hybrid generative texture model from the
    user's iconic_data object: a torch GAN learns the sample-level
    covariate, exposure, and outcome structure, while a Gaussian copula
    model learns the full feature-level joint distribution of the
    mediator panel (marginal distributions via empirical CDF with
    parametric fallback, cross-feature dependence via Gaussian copula).
    This ensures synthetic mediator texture preserves realistic marginal
    shapes (skewness, tails, bounded support) and cross-feature
    correlations from the user's cohort. The negative-control panel
    remains generated by a pluggable nc_model with residual correlation
    injection. The torch package is required (no multivariate-normal
    fallback). The
    infer_confounding() function estimates held-fixed confounding
    parameters (confounding strength, mediator-outcome confounding, NC
    coverage, number of latent confounders) from the data via
    estimator-gap and parallel-analysis methods, with a confounding =
    "inferred" option in iconic_sensitivity() and iconic_prospect(). The
    modular API: iconic_data() constructs a standardized data object;
    iconic_diagnose() runs instrument-strength checks (partial
    F-statistics), negative-control validity screens (A1:
    W-perp-Z-given-C, A2: W-perp-G-given-C, A2': W-perp-Gm-given-C with
    BH-FDR control), and path-completeness assessment; iconic_estimate()
    fits all eligible estimators (UNADJ, DIRECT, COCA, IV2SLS, PGC,
    IV2SLS2, PGC2, PGC2Gm); iconic_sensitivity() generates a degradation
    surface over instrument-independence violations (rho_G1 x rho_G2 grid)
    calibrated to the user's data; and iconic_recommend() ranks estimators
    by identification strength (tier A: identified, B: negative-control,
    C: instrument-based, D: bias reference) and robustness. For users
    without instruments or negative controls, iconic_prospect() performs
    prospective analysis showing how estimates would change if such data
    were collected. The package includes publication figure plotting
    functions (ggplot2 + patchwork) that reproduce all manuscript figures
    from package computation results. The package retains its full
    simulation benchmarking infrastructure, including mediation estimators
    for natural direct (NDE) and natural indirect (NIE) effects under
    mediator-outcome confounding, a weak-instrument guard (Stock & Yogo,
    2005), pleiotropy sensitivity sweeps, and empirical negative-control
    validity diagnostics. The data-generating process supports imperfect
    instrument independence (rho_G1, rho_G2, rho_pop, separate_U, omega_1,
    omega_2) and a tunable instrument strength parameter (gamma_G). The
    data-generating process also supports block-diagonal feature
    correlations (feat_cor) modelling co-expression modules, sweepable
    via sweep_mediation_param("feat_cor", ...) with a dedicated
    plot_feature_correlation_sweep() figure function. All
    computationally intensive functions (iconic_estimate,
    iconic_sensitivity, iconic_prospect, iconic_diagnose,
    infer_confounding, sweep_nc_validity, sweep_instrument_strength,
    and the exported analysis functions) support multi-core
    parallelization via the n_cores argument, using
    parallel::mclapply on Unix and PSOCK clusters on Windows, with
    message()-based progress milestones. Version 0.9.4 adds
    time-to-event (survival) outcome support: iconic_data() accepts
    outcome_type = "survival" with surv_time and surv_event arguments,
    and iconic_estimate() fits all estimators on the Cox log-hazard-ratio
    scale (effect_scale = "loghr") or the restricted-mean-survival-time
    scale (effect_scale = "rmst") via two-stage predictor substitution
    (2SPS), where first-stage regressions remain OLS and only the
    outcome stage switches to coxph. Mediation (NDE/NIE) is supported
    on both scales; the RMST scale provides exact product-of-
    coefficients decomposition while the log-HR scale is approximate
    due to non-collapsibility. The simulation DGP, sensitivity
    analysis, and prospective analysis functions all support
    outcome_type = "survival". COCA is structurally incompatible with
    survival outcomes and returns NA with an informative reason.
